QUESTION 1

CAR DEALERSHIP

A typical car dealership sells and orders cars based on their stock. Once a delaership runs out of cars, it is time to order some cars from the inventory of the car manufacturer, which itself churns out a specific number of cars (a figure predefined based on some supply-demand statistics by a bunch of “has-been” MBA graduates). Your job is to implement a rough algorithm that will oversee this. To keep things simple, we will sell only a generic car model across ‘x’ dealerships...a weird car brand some might say!

Given:
n = dealership stock m = inventory capacity

Solution:

int dealers = x;

sem_t fullStock[x] = {n} // array of sem init to max cars in stock sem_t emptyStock[x] = {0}

sem_t fullInventory = {m} // sem init to max inventory size sem_t emptyInventory

num_cars[x] = {n}; tot_cars = m;

buyCars(int c, int d) // c = number of cars customer wants to buy
// d = car dealership number
{
for(i = 0; i < c; i++)
{
sem_wait(&fullStock[d]); sem_wait(&buyerMutex);
purchase(); // complete the purchase of a car num_cars[x]--;
sem_post(&buyerMutex); sem_post(&emptyStock[d]);
}
}

dealer(int d)

{
while(1)
{
sem_wait(&emptyStock[d]); sem_wait(&buyerMutex); if(d_cars[d] == 0)
getCars(d); sem_post(&buyerMutex)
// we post on sem fullStock n times to make sure the semantics of our sem (range n) is not violated
for(i = 0; i < n; i++)
{
sem_post(&fullStock[d]);
}
}
}
getCars(int d)
{
for(i = 0; i < n; i++)
{
sem_wait(&fullInventory); sem_wait(&sellerMutex); tot_cars --;
num_cars[d] ++; sem_post(&sellerMutex); sem_post(&emptyInventory);
}
}
manufacturer()
{
while(1)
{
sem_wait(&emptyInventory); sem_wait(&sellerMutex);
if (tot_cars == 0)
 tot_cars += m; // add m cars to inventory sem_post(&sellerMutex);
// we post on sem fullInventory m times to make sure the semantics of our sem (range m) is not violated
for(i = 0; i < m; i++)
{
sem_post(&fullInventory);
}
}
}

