Consider the below statements:

The atomic fetch-and-set x, y instruction retrieves the previous value of x from y and unconditionally sets the memory location x to 1 without allowing any other access during the process. Take into account the following binary semaphore implementation of the P and V functions.
AcquireLock(L){
 	while (Fetch_And_Add(L,1))
 	L = 1;
   }
  ReleaseLock(L){
 	L = 0;
   }This implementation (A) fails as L can overflow
fails as L can take on a non-zero value when the lock is actually available
works correctly but may starve some processes (D) works correctly without starvation

Answer (B)
Take closer look the below while loop. while (Fetch_And_Add(L,1))
L = 1; // A waiting process can be here just after
// the lock is released, and can make L = 1.
Consider a situation where a process has just released the lock and made L = 0. Let there be one more process waiting for the lock, means executing the AcquireLock() function. Just after the L was made 0, let the waiting processes executed the line L = 1. Now, the lock is available and L = 1. Since L is 1, the waiting process (and any other future coming processes) can not come out of the while loop.
The above problem can be resolved by changing the AcuireLock() to following. AcquireLock(L){
while (Fetch_And_Add(L,1))
{ // Do Nothing }

}

The atomic fetch-and-set x, y instruction fetches the previous value of x from y and unconditionally sets the memory location x to 1 without allowing any other access during the process. Take into account the following binary semaphore implementation of the P and V functions.
void P (binary_semaphore *s) { unsigned y;
unsigned *x = &(s->value); do {
fetch-and-set x, y;
} while (y);

}

void V (binary_semaphore *s) { S->value = 0;
} The implementation may not work if context switching is disabled in P.Explain?


Let us talk about the operation P(). It stores the value of s in x, then it fetches the old value of x, stores it in y and sets x as 1. The while loop of a process will continue forever if some other process doesn’t execute V() and sets the value of s as 0. If context switching is disabled in P, the while loop will run forever as no other process will be able to execute V().

The P and V operations on counting semaphores are defined as 

 P(s): s = s-1;
if s < 0 then wait; V(s) : s = s-1;
ifs <= 0 then wakeup a process waiting on s;
Assume that Pb and Vb the wait and signal opeíations on binaíy semaphoíes aíe píovided. ľwo binaíy semaphoíes Xb and Yb aíe used to implement the semaphoíe opeíations P(s) and V(s) as follows:

P(s): Pb (Xb);
S = s - 1;
if(s < 0){
Vb(Xb);
Pb(Yb);
}
Else Vb (Xb); V(s): Pb (Xb);
S = s + 1;
if(s <= 0) Vb(Yb); Vb(Xb);
ľhe initial values of Xb and Yb aíe íespectively?
Ans: Explanation: Suppose Xb = 0, then because of P(s): Pb(Xb) opeíation, Xb will be
-1 and píocess will get blocked as it will enteí into waiting section.


So, Xb will be one.


Suppose s=2(means 2 píocess aíe accessing shaíed íesouíce), taking Xb as 1,


fiíst P(s): Pb(Xb) opeíation will make Xb as zeío. s will be 1 and ľhen Vb(Xb) opeíation will be executed which will incíease the count of Xb as one. ľhen same píocess will be íepeated making Xb as one and s as zeío.


Now suppose one moíe píocess comes, then Xb will be 0 but s will be -1 which will make this píocess go into loop (s <0) and will íesult into calling Vb(Xb) and Pb(Yb) opeíations. Vb(Xb) will íesult into Xb as 1 and Pb(Yb) will íesult into decíementing the value of Yb.
case 1: if Yb has value as 0, it will be -1 and it will go into waiting and will be blocked.total 2 píocess will access shaíed íesouíce (accoíding to counting 
semaphoíe, max 3 píocess can access shaíed íesouíce) and value of s is -1 means only 1 píocess will be waiting foí íesouíces and just now, one píocess got blocked. So it is still tíue.


case 2: if Yb has value as 1, it will be 0. ľotal 3 píocess will access shaíed íesouíce (accoíding to counting semaphoíe, max 2 píocess can access shaíed íesouíce) and value of s is -1 means only 1 píocess will be waiting foí íesouíces and but theíe is no píocess waiting foí íesouíces.So it is false.

A cíucial section of code must be accessed by two píocesses, P1 and P2. ľhink about the píocesses' use of the following synchíonisation   constíuct:
/* P1 */ while(true){ want s1=true;
while(wants2 == true){
/* Critical Section */
wants1 = false;
}
/* Reminder Section */
}
/* P2 */ while(true){ want s2=true;
while(wants1 == true){
/* Critical Section */
Wants2 = false;
}
/* Reminder Section */
}
Heíe wants1 and wants2 aíe shaíed vaíiables, which aíe initialized to false. Which one of the following statements is ľRUE about the above constíuct?
It does not ensure mutual exclusion.
It does not ensure bounded waiting.
It requires that processes enter the critical section in strict alternation.
It does not prevent deadlocks, but ensures mutual exclusion. Answer: (D)

Explanation:
Bounded waiting :There exists a bound, or limit, on the number of times other processes are allowed to enter their critical sections after a process has made request to enter its critical section and before that request is granted.

mutual exclusion prevents simultaneous access to a shared resource. This concept is used in concurrent programming with a critical section, a piece of code in which processes or threads access a shared resource.

he following two functions P1 and P2 that share a variable B with an initial value of 2 execute concurrently-
P1(){ C=B-1; B=2*C;
} P2(){
D=2*B;
B=D-1;
}
What are the distinct values of B?
Ans-> Case-01: The execution order of the instructions may be- P1(1), P1(2), P2(1), P2(2)
In this case, Final value of B = 3
Case-02: The execution order of the instructions may be- P2(1), P2(2), P1(1), P1(2)
In this case, Final value of B = 4
Case-03:The execution order of the instructions may be- P1(1), P2(1), P2(2), P1(2)
In this case, Final value of B = 2
Case-04: The execution order of the instructions may be- P2(1), P1(1), P1(2), P2(2)
In this case, Final value of B = 3

Case-05: The execution order of the instructions may be- P1(1), P2(1), P1(2), P2(2)
In this case, Final value of B = 3
Case-06: The execution order of the instructions may be- P2(1), P1(1), P2(2), P1(2)
In this case, Final value of B = 2
From here, Distinct values that may be produced are 2, 3 and 4. Therefore, Number of distinct values that may be produced = 3

In the following code,

pthread_mutex_t lock = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER; pthread_cond_t init = PTHREAD_COND_INITIALIZER; Pthread_mutex_lock(&lock);
while (initialized == 0) Pthread_cond_wait(&init, &lock); Pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
The code text to see whether the variable initialized has been set to 0, then calls the wait routine to sleep until the other thread wakes it up.
The following is the awaking code, Pthread_mutex_lock(&lock); initialized = 1; Pthread_cond_signal(&init); Pthread_mutex_unlock(&lock);
You might have noticed that the wait call takes a lock as its second input, but the signal call only takes a condition. Explain.


Answer:

The reason is that the wait call, in addition to putting the calling thread to sleep, releases the lock when putting told the caller to sleep.
Otherwise how could another thread acquire the lock and signal it to wake up?
However, before returning after being woken, the pthread_cond_wait() re-acquires the lock, thus ensuring that anytime the waiting thread is running between the lock acquired at the beginning of the wait sequence and the lock release at the end, it holds the lock.

