In the dinning philosophers problem there is a round table and N philosophers are
sitting with N forks between them. The task given to each philosopher is that he have to
pick both the fork on the left and right but with the constraint that if each philosopher
first picks the fork an right(lets say) then it will cause deadlock while waiting for the other
fork.Write a possible solution for this problem

Ans:
A variable state is associated with each philosopher, and can be one of EATING
(holdingboth forks) or THINKING (when not eating). Further, a condition variable is
associated with each philosopher to make them sleep and wake them up when needed.
Each philosopher must call the pickup function before eating, and putdown function when
done. Both these functions use a mutex to change states only when both forks are available.
bothForksFree(i):
return (state[leftNbr(i)] != EATING && state[rightNbr(i)] != EATING)
putdown(i):
lock(mutex)
state[i] = THINKING
if(bothForksFree(leftNbr(i)))
signal(leftNbr(i))
if(bothForksFree(rightNbr(i)))
signal(rightNbr(i))
unlock(mutex)
pickup(i):
lock(mutex)
while(!bothForksFree(i))
wait(condvar[i])
state[i] = EATING
unlock(mutex)

Consider a system consisting of n processes (P0, P1, ………Pn -1) each process has a
segment of code which is known as critical section in which the process may be changing
common variable, updating a table, writing a file and so on. The important feature of the
system is that when the process is executing in its critical section no other process is to be
allowed to execute in its critical section. The execution of critical sections by the processes
is a mutually exclusive. The critical section problem is to design a protocol that the process
can use to cooperate each process must request permission to enter its critical section.
The section of code implementing this request is the entry section. The critical section is
followed on exit section. The remaining code is the remainder section
Example:
While (1)
{
Entry Section;
Critical Section;
Exit Section;
Remainder Section;
}

Ans:
A solution to the critical section problem must satisfy the following three conditions.

Vaibhav, Suraj and Uday go to a Chinese restaurant. But they go there at 8 pm (Busy time). So, waiter apologize and says he can provide only 2 pairs of chopsticks (i.e., four chopsticks), which need to be share between 3 friends.  Now, Vaibhav come up with an idea. He suggests to put all four chopsticks in an empty glass at center table & each diner should obey the following protocol: 
while ( ! had_enough_to_eat() ) 
{ 
aquire_one_chopstick(); 
aquire_one_chopstick(); 
eat(); 
release_one_chopstick(); 
release_one_chopstick(); 
} 
Can this plan lead to a deadlock? Explain your answer. 

Answer: No. Deadlock cannot occur because there are enough chopsticks to guarantee that at least one of the 3 diners will be able to get the 2 chopsticks that he/she needs. Once that diner finishes, he/she will release their chopsticks for someone else. So, everyone will finish.

(Read above problem first) 
Suppose, now instead of 3 diners, we have 'D' number of diners. Furthermore, each diner may acquire different number of chopsticks. Let`s say 'C' number of total chopstick will satisfy the needs of all diners (e.g., for 4 people (D=4), assuming each require 3 chopsticks, then 'C' will be C = 3*4=12). 
Now, each dinners protocol is displayed below: 

int s; 
int num_sticks = my_chopsticks_requirement(); 
while (! had_enough_to_eat()) 
{ 
for (s=0; s <num_sticks; s++) 
{ 
acquire_one_chopstick(); 
} 
eat(); 
for (s=0; s <num_sticks; s++) 
{ 
release_one_chopstick(); 
}
} 
what is the smallest number of chopsticks (in terms of C and D), needed to ensure that deadlock cannot occur? Explain your answer. 
Ans: 
C-D+1. 
This guarantees that every dinner cat get all but one of chopstick it needs (C-D), with one additional chopstick to guarantee that at least one dinner gets all of the chopstick it needs. 
Example, Suppose D=2 and diner_1 require 1 chopstick and diner_2 require 11 chopsticks. Therefore C = 12 
So, C-D+1= 12-2+1=11 
So maximum chopstick requirement is satisfied.

